 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} = 3.$" 13194139534209 true] [17592186171740 1100 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3} = \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} + \\displaystyle{\\left[ \\phantom{3 \\choose 3}\\right]}\n$$" 13194139534209 true] [17592186171740 1100 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n$\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3} = \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} + \\displaystyle{\\left[\\phantom{3 \\choose 3}\\right]}$" 13194139534209 false] [17592186171740 1145 "To find $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{4} \\choose {\\color{blue}3}}$, we need to find the number that is located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}3$ of Pascal's triangle.\n\n**Important:** Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0$.\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0$.\n\nThe location of $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{0}{0}); (\\frac{1}{0})\\begin{vmatrix}(\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\ (\\frac{2}{0})\\end{vmatrix}(\\frac{2}{1})\\begin{vmatrix}(\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\ (\\frac{3}{0})\\end{vmatrix}(\\frac{3}{1})\\begin{vmatrix}(\\frac{3}{2}) & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\ (\\frac{4}{0}) & (\\frac{4}{1})\\end{vmatrix}(\\frac{4}{2})\\begin{vmatrix}(4 3) & (\\frac{4}{4}) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\\begin{vmatrix}⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\n$$\n\nNow, $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3} = \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} + \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\displaystyle{3 \\choose 3}$." 13194139534209 false] [17592186171740 1145 "To find $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{4} \\choose {\\color{blue}3}},$ we need to find the number that is located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important:** Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\frac{4}{0}) &  & (\\frac{4}{1}) &  & (\\frac{4}{2}) &  & (\\frac{4}{3}) &  & (\\frac{4}{4}) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3} = \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} + \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\displaystyle{3 \\choose 3}.$" 13194139534209 true] [17592186171741 1100 "Determine the value of $\\displaystyle{5 \\choose 4}$." 13194139534209 false] [17592186171741 1100 "Determine the value of $\\displaystyle{5 \\choose 4}.$" 13194139534209 true] [17592186171741 1145 "The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n = 5$ and $r = 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\frac{5!}{(5-4)!\\cdot 4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4!}{1!\\cdot 4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534209 true] [17592186171741 1145 "The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\, r!}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n = 5$ and $r = 4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5}{4}) &= (5!)/((5 - 4)! \\cdot 4!) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4!}{1! \\cdot 4!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4!}{1 \\cdot 4!} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534209 false] [17592186171887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282419 13194139534209 true] [17592186171887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282426 13194139534209 true] [17592186171887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282433 13194139534209 true] [17592186171887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282482 13194139534209 true] [17592186171888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282440 13194139534209 true] [17592186171888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282447 13194139534209 true] [17592186171888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282489 13194139534209 true] [17592186171888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282496 13194139534209 true] [17592186171889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282454 13194139534209 true] [17592186171889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282461 13194139534209 true] [17592186171889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282468 13194139534209 true] [17592186171889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282475 13194139534209 true] [17592186171889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282503 13194139534209 true] [17592186172520 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534209 true] [17592186172520 1100 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{1})$." 13194139534209 false] [17592186172520 1100 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{1}).$" 13194139534209 true] [17592186172520 1145 "To find the value of $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $3$ and position $1$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\